"Partitions a rectangle by two categorical variables so cell area represents their joint frequency.\n\nUse when: A contingency table needs both marginal sizes and conditional composition. Readers should see associations between two categorical dimensions.\n\nAvoid when: Many sparse categories produce unreadable slivers or exact differences are the priority.\n\nVisual grammar: Column width encodes the first category’s marginal share. Within-column segment height encodes conditional share, so rectangle area encodes joint share.\n\nBuild: Count observations in every category pair and calculate marginal totals. Allocate column widths by first-category totals. Split each column by second-category proportions and label only cells with adequate room.\n\nCheck: Check all cell areas sum to the declared total or 100%. Verify each column’s heights sum to one. Distinguish structural zeros from missing or unobserved combinations.\n\nPitfalls: Vertical boundaries need not align across columns because conditional shares differ. Area comparisons are imprecise; use a contingency heatmap when small differences matter.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic subscription tier and device category, with column width representing tier size and segment height representing device share.\n\nAlternatives: 100% stacked bar chart, Heatmap, Stacked bar chart."
